+SD cards
+========
+
+The ``sd-card`` device models an SD memory card on an SD bus. On PCI machines,
+attach each card to its own ``sdhci-pci`` controller. For example:
+
+.. code-block:: console
+
+    qemu-img create -f raw sd.img 8G
+    qemu-system-x86_64 -machine pc -m 512 \
+        -drive file=sd.img,format=raw,if=none,id=sd-image \
+        -device sdhci-pci,id=sdhci,sd-spec-version=2,bootindex=1 \
+        -device sd-card,drive=sd-image,bus=pci.0/sdhci/sd-bus
+
+Unlike eMMC, the card uses the whole image as its user area, without boot or
+RPMB partition offsets.
+
+The ``spec_version`` property accepts ``2`` (SD v2.00) or ``3`` (SD v3.01,
+the default). Capacity selects standard or high-capacity addressing. Images
+up to 2 GiB must have a power-of-two size; the smallest exactly representable
+capacity is 256 KiB. Larger images use 512 KiB units and the SDHC/SDXC CSD
+encoding represents at most 2 TiB. Create partition tables and filesystems
+inside the guest.
+
+The optional ``cid`` property accepts exactly 32 hexadecimal characters:
+
+.. code-block:: console
+
+    -device sd-card,drive=sd-image,cid=03534453443332478535bca2a70195df
+
+Omitting it retains the existing identity. A configured value is reapplied
+on reset, preserves all supplied bytes and does not modify the image. The
+existing CID migration state is unchanged; configure the same value and SD
+specification at both migration endpoints. A realized device's CID property
+cannot be changed.
+
+Standard SDHCI response registers omit the final CRC/end-bit byte. Linux
+therefore reports ``00`` for the last byte in
+``/sys/block/mmcblkN/device/cid`` with this controller, even if the configured
+CID ends differently. This is a controller/driver limitation, not a property
+of SD cards themselves.
+
+The optional ``sdhci-pci.bootindex`` property associates boot priority with
+that controller's card. It defaults to ``-1`` (no priority). Guest firmware
+must contain SD/MMC boot drivers and understand QEMU boot order. SeaBIOS and
+PVE's 4 MiB UEFI firmware support this configuration; older 2 MiB UEFI builds
+may not include the required drivers. This property does not alter the
+default controller model or its migration format.
+
+Adding or removing SD card devices, changing capacity or changing identity
+requires a cold restart of the virtual machine. SD bus devices are not
+hotpluggable. SD cards have no eMMC RPMB key/counter state; ordinary disk
+backup, snapshot and migration mechanisms apply.
